{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1489760-few-questions-about-knocked-down-tilt","id":1489760,"title":"Few questions about Knocked Down Tilt","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-08-11T10:41:05-07:00","id":1489760,"title":"Few questions about Knocked Down Tilt","content":"So, I'm quite confused about this Tilt. As far as I understand, there are multiple ways to cause it, with those two being the main ones: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>If you manage to inflict two <b>Leg Wrack</b> tilts simultaneously, your victim falls prone and gains <b>Knocked Down</b>. I can only assume that penalties do not stack, so the victim would still only have -2 to Defense and Physical rolls, not -4.</li> <li>If you attack with the intent to inflict Knocked Down, you must halve your successes, and take -2 penalty to target legs in case you're attacking unarmed or your weapon modifier is below +2 (melee) or +3 (ranged). If your attack still hits (so it deals at least 1 point of damage, even bashing), your victim gains Knocked Down.</li> </ol><br /> In either case, the victim is also considered <b>Prone</b>, so any Ranged Attacks gain -2 penalty, while Melee Attacks gain +2 modifier.<br /> <br /> Text of the tilt is pretty confusing, so did I get that right?<br /> If so, then I'd like to clarify something else: how exactly do you end this Tilt without wasting your turn? Obviously you can wait until your turn and <b>Stand Up</b>, using your instant action and technically eating away your turn. But the Tilt also mentions that: <span style=\"color:#0000CD\">&quot;</span><i><span style=\"color:#0000CD\">A character who hasn\u2019t yet acted can make a Dexterity + Athletics roll, minus any weapon modifier, instead of her normal action. If successful, she avoids the effects of this Tilt&quot;</span>. </i>So, it effectively lets you &quot;Stand Up&quot; before your turn and avoid any nasty +2 Melee Attacks against your -2 Defense that you could have suffered otherwise, but at a cost of your next turn, right? Also, whose Weapon Modifier do you subtract from your Dexterity + Athletics roll, yours or the emeny's? And how does it interact with &quot;<b>Knockdown</b>&quot; weapon tag from <b>Hurt Locker</b> that lets you:<span style=\"color:#0000CD\"><i> &quot;Double the weapon bonus for purposes of the Knocked Down Tilt&quot;</i></span>?<br /> <br /> If all this stuff is correct, doesn't it seem a bit overpowered? I takes just one damage to inflict Knocked Down, and you still deal damage to your enemy while effectively taking away their turn by forcing them to &quot;Stand Up&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27533,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27533-vargock","user":"Vargock","date":". 08-11-2022, 01:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27533-vargock","name":"Vargock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27533_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-11T11:50:48-07:00","id":1489773,"title":"","content":"When you have Leg Wrack on both legs (making the character Knocked Down), any Physical roll that requires movement (or Defense, such as Dodge) is reduced to a chance dice, Speed is reduced to 1, all attacks receive -2 penalty.<br /> <br /> When determining the penalty to keep your footing, I think its intended to use the attacker's weapon damage modifier. This probably represents rebalancing despite the damage affecting the limbs, momentum of the attack or disorientation from the blow.<br /> <br /> While inflicting Knocked Down is very useful, it requires some work. You need either less concealable weapons, ones that have been modified, an above average level of skill in your preferred combat range or supernatural augmentation. Even then, surprise and reaction speed determine whether or not you can take the most advantage of the action denial.<br /> <br /> So it can be very powerful, but there are a few factors to account for. Another example is how much damage covering fire can do to an enemy group, while also locking their movement for other characters to flank or AoE. But that requires an automatic weapon and will eat through your bullets very quickly. Then there is always the absurd levels of damage a character with high stealth can do by denying Defense or waiting for the right situation to deliver a Killing Blow. Fire and electricity simply do automatic damage, regardless of defense, making them exceptionally powerful tools for an ambush.<br /> <br /> A sniper who takes the time to aim properly can probably stun at best, which is far deadlier, or knock down with a casual shot.<br /> <br /> There are tons of devastating tactics, even before getting into the sheer utility of fighting styles. Combat in CofD can be remarkably dangerous and deadly, which encourages constant use of Willpower to survive, meaning its emotionally draining for characters as well.<br /> <br /> The fact the major splats, with the right abilities, can walk out of those mundane combat scenarios not terribly worse for wear is a reason Hunters have to use every dirty trick in the book to stand a chance. Eventually they need to write a new book, just so their opponents can't predict them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":24280,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","user":"KaiserAfini","date":". 08-11-2022, 07:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-11T17:01:51-07:00","id":1489813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vargock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1489760-few-questions-about-knocked-down-tilt#post1489760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you manage to inflict two <b>Leg Wrack</b> tilts simultaneously, your victim falls prone and gains <b>Knocked Down</b>. I can only assume that penalties do not stack, so the victim would still only have -2 to Defense and Physical rolls, not -4.</div> </div> </div> </div>The modifier for having no unWracked Legs, as Afini points out, is substantially worse than a -4 penalty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If so, then I'd like to clarify something else: how exactly do you end this Tilt without wasting your turn? </div> </div> </div>By considering not being stuck prone in the middle of a fight more of a benefit than a waste. Recall that using your turn doesn't use up your Speed \u2014 you can still move to put some distance between you and the opposition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, whose Weapon Modifier do you subtract from your Dexterity + Athletics roll, yours or the emeny's? </div> </div> </div>Your own. Stronger weapons tend to be more unwieldy (hence the Initiative modifier that tends to scale with Damage) and dropping your weapon is a reflexive action.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If all this stuff is correct, doesn't it seem a bit overpowered? I takes just one damage to inflict Knocked Down, and you still deal damage to your enemy while effectively taking away their turn by forcing them to &quot;Stand Up&quot;. </div> </div> </div>This is a system where most fights with nonlethal stakes end when one side takes less than four points of damage and combat is designed to chew through Willpower from nonspecialists. Inflicting Knocked Down through targeted attacks to the legs requires you to overcome a -2 penalty on top of any Defense the target possesses, and if you're trying to achieve it through inflicting Leg Wrack twice you need to overcome the target's Stamina on two separate attacks with this restriction. Attempting to inflict the Tilt directly, meanwhile, gives your opponent the chance to roll to avoid the Tilt, which itself is an arguably suboptimal response compared to simply Dodging the attack, but even if they <i>do</i> end up on the ground, they're not subject to the additional complications of Leg Wrack and can still fight back with only a moderate extra penalty.<br /> <br /> Like, yes, if you can deprive the opposition of the ability to stay on their feet you have a significant advantage in a fight. Unless you are attempting to murder them utterly or they are some kind of bloodthirsty monster, the prerequisites for doing so are negligibly different from getting them to give up by hitting them with a boomstick big enough to put them halfway to death's door, and in the latter case you probably don't want to be taking your chances doing less damage unless you're very sure they can't hit back without getting up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}